00:03:35.100 --> 00:03:36.920
Towards the end of Tuesday's
class,

00:03:36.920 --> 00:03:40.900
this was one of the graphs that
we looked at quite a bit.

00:03:40.900 --> 00:03:46.220
It was a scatterplot on which
the x-axis represented the time

00:03:46.220 --> 00:03:50.080
we left to go to work in the
morning, and the y-axis

00:03:50.080 --> 00:03:53.160
represented how long it took to
get to work.

00:03:53.160 --> 00:03:55.440
So each one of these data
points,

00:03:55.440 --> 00:03:58.160
or each one of the points on
this scatterplot reflects one

00:03:58.160 --> 00:04:02.560
row of some real person's log
that they took every day,

00:04:02.560 --> 00:04:05.460
that they wrote down every day
they went to work.

00:04:05.460 --> 00:04:10.060
And our goal was to try and
build the model that would allow

00:04:10.060 --> 00:04:13.860
us to predict commute times in
the future.

00:04:13.860 --> 00:04:16.980
And what that model would do is
that it would allow me to plug

00:04:16.980 --> 00:04:19.600
in the time I want to leave in
the morning.

00:04:19.600 --> 00:04:23.740
And it would give me its
prediction for how long it

00:04:23.740 --> 00:04:27.060
thinks it'll take for me to get
to school if I leave at that

00:04:27.060 --> 00:04:27.960
time.

00:04:27.960 --> 00:04:30.660
And that model will be trained

00:04:30.660 --> 00:04:34.140
and will learn a pattern from
this historical data that we've

00:04:34.140 --> 00:04:37.220
already collected.
But ultimately, our eventual

00:04:37.220 --> 00:04:40.800
goal is for the model to work
well on future data when we

00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:43.660
don't exactly know what the
commute times are going to look

00:04:43.660 --> 00:04:44.660
like.

00:04:45.180 --> 00:04:47.740
Here's one thing I should have
emphasized on Tuesday that I

00:04:47.740 --> 00:04:50.440
didn't.
There's an assumption about the

00:04:50.440 --> 00:04:54.060
world we need to make in order
for this process to yield

00:04:54.060 --> 00:04:55.120
something useful.

00:04:55.120 --> 00:04:56.400
That is,

00:04:56.400 --> 00:04:58.260
we're using this historical
data.

00:04:58.260 --> 00:05:01.760
What must be true about the
future in order for the model

00:05:01.760 --> 00:05:04.700
that we make using this
historical data to still be

00:05:04.700 --> 00:05:05.700
useful?

00:05:07.760 --> 00:05:09.520
Gavin.
The future has to be similar to

00:05:09.520 --> 00:05:10.480
the past? Yeah,

00:05:10.480 --> 00:05:13.420
future commute patterns need to
look similar to past commute

00:05:13.420 --> 00:05:14.420
patterns.

00:05:14.460 --> 00:05:18.880
But if they open a brand new
Autobahn-type highway with no

00:05:18.880 --> 00:05:21.080
traffic on it, maybe all of
these commute times will go

00:05:21.080 --> 00:05:21.920
down.

00:05:21.920 --> 00:05:23.880
Or if they close all of the
highways,

00:05:23.880 --> 00:05:25.380
or who knows?

00:05:25.380 --> 00:05:29.420
If any aspect of the system in
which this data was collected

00:05:29.420 --> 00:05:30.420
changes,

00:05:30.720 --> 00:05:34.500
the data that we used from the
past may not represent data in

00:05:34.500 --> 00:05:36.340
the future.
And so the models we learn on it

00:05:36.340 --> 00:05:38.520
may not be representative.

00:05:38.520 --> 00:05:39.860
Does that sound reasonable?

00:05:39.860 --> 00:05:40.560
OK.

00:05:40.560 --> 00:05:45.500
And this is why, putting this in
context of things like ChatGPT,

00:05:45.500 --> 00:05:48.440
I actually got a survey from
some linguistics researcher at

00:05:48.440 --> 00:05:51.440
another university who asked
questions about this,

00:05:51.500 --> 00:05:53.000
about how

00:05:53.500 --> 00:05:55.760
there are lots of different
dialects of English.

00:05:55.760 --> 00:05:58.740
And many of us in this room, or
at least at the university, we

00:05:58.740 --> 00:06:00.800
speak American English.
But there's British English,

00:06:00.800 --> 00:06:01.700
Canadian English.

00:06:01.700 --> 00:06:03.380
And different parts of the world
have their own dialects of

00:06:03.380 --> 00:06:06.080
English.
But because the creators of the

00:06:06.080 --> 00:06:09.020
large language models primarily
think in American English,

00:06:09.020 --> 00:06:12.120
and so the models are trained
and fine-tuned to speak that

00:06:12.120 --> 00:06:14.370
sort of dialect,

00:06:14.600 --> 00:06:16.600
then people in other parts of
the world who have their own

00:06:16.600 --> 00:06:19.420
versions of English that don't
sound like the way that our

00:06:19.420 --> 00:06:20.740
English sounds,

00:06:20.740 --> 00:06:24.040
or the way that English sounds
here in Michigan,

00:06:24.040 --> 00:06:28.000
are sort of biased towards our
way of speaking English, even if

00:06:28.000 --> 00:06:29.880
it's not necessarily right, it's
just our way.

00:06:29.880 --> 00:06:31.060
So, like,

00:06:31.060 --> 00:06:34.220
for example, you could imagine
someone being in a country where

00:06:34.220 --> 00:06:36.440
English isn't the first
language, but it's a popular

00:06:36.440 --> 00:06:40.000
second language, and you may put
in, like, an essay that you

00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:41.520
wrote and say,

00:06:41.520 --> 00:06:43.880
I don't know, fix the grammar
here.

00:06:43.880 --> 00:06:46.080
You could imagine it fixing a
lot of things that aren't

00:06:46.080 --> 00:06:48.940
actually issues, they're just
not as American as they could

00:06:48.940 --> 00:06:49.540
be.

00:06:49.540 --> 00:06:51.200
And why is that happening?

00:06:51.200 --> 00:06:53.600
It's because the training data
doesn't reflect the reality in

00:06:53.600 --> 00:06:54.900
which the model is trying to be
used.

00:06:54.900 --> 00:06:59.240
So this is a problem even for
large systems that you work with

00:06:59.240 --> 00:07:00.740
every day.

00:07:00.860 --> 00:07:05.560
So our goal here is to try and
predict commute times as best as

00:07:05.560 --> 00:07:07.060
we could.

