So this simple linear regression
model has two parameters and

00:12:46.520 --> 00:12:49.800
given a data set if we choose to
use the simple linear regression

00:12:49.800 --> 00:12:52.620
model the question is well what
should we set these parameters

00:12:52.620 --> 00:12:56.280
to so that the resulting model
fits the data well and what you

00:12:56.280 --> 00:13:01.080
can see here is that some
choices of parameters do an okay

00:13:01.080 --> 00:13:06.880
job like 160 for the intercept W
naught

00:13:06.880 --> 00:13:11.480
and negative 10 for the slope W1
and some do an atrocious job,

00:13:11.480 --> 00:13:13.200
like 20 and 5.

00:13:13.200 --> 00:13:15.540
And so what we're trying to get
at is, well, how do we know

00:13:15.540 --> 00:13:18.540
which combination of parameters
would do the best job?

00:13:18.580 --> 00:13:21.000
Ideally, there's a way to do
this in a more principled

00:13:21.000 --> 00:13:25.420
manner than just looking at the
page and eyeballing it.

00:13:25.420 --> 00:13:29.460
Especially because we will soon
enter a territory where we have

00:13:29.460 --> 00:13:33.740
more than one input variable or
more than one feature.

00:13:33.740 --> 00:13:36.600
What if we have 10 features? So
I'm using 10 input variables to

00:13:36.600 --> 00:13:38.300
predict how long my commute is
going to take.

00:13:38.300 --> 00:13:39.760
Well, then,

00:13:39.760 --> 00:13:43.580
I can't even draw the graph, at
least not natively, right?

00:13:43.580 --> 00:13:48.540
Because I would need 10 axes to
represent my 10 input variables,

00:13:48.540 --> 00:13:50.840
and then an 11th axis

00:13:50.840 --> 00:13:53.500
to represent the actual commute
time.

00:13:53.500 --> 00:13:57.000
So I would need to be able to
visualize in 11 dimensional

00:13:57.000 --> 00:13:58.000
space.

00:13:58.000 --> 00:14:00.340
But we're just humans, we can
only visualize in three

00:14:00.340 --> 00:14:03.600
dimensional space, which is why
very quickly we will no longer

00:14:03.600 --> 00:14:06.300
be able to directly visualize
the quantities we're working

00:14:06.300 --> 00:14:07.660
with.
So we need to come up with

00:14:07.660 --> 00:14:10.480
mathematical solutions that
don't rely on being able to see.

00:14:11.360 --> 00:14:14.360
So we'll build intuition in two
dimensions and three dimensions,

00:14:14.360 --> 00:14:18.860
and then come up with general
solutions that work beyond that.

00:14:18.940 --> 00:14:20.900
So this is an overview of the
simple linear regression model,

00:14:20.900 --> 00:14:24.020
but we're going to focus on the
constant model.

00:14:24.020 --> 00:14:25.700
The goal for the rest of today

00:14:25.700 --> 00:14:29.200
is how do we figure out what the
best constant is?

00:14:29.200 --> 00:14:31.240
What is the best value of w?

00:14:31.240 --> 00:14:32.880
All we're going to do for the
rest of the day,

00:14:32.880 --> 00:14:36.060
what is the best value of w for
the constant model?

00:14:36.060 --> 00:14:37.560
Sound good?

00:14:37.820 --> 00:14:43.580
So what I want to do is turn
this into kind of like a game,

00:14:43.580 --> 00:14:47.420
where for every value of w,

00:14:47.420 --> 00:14:50.360
I give it a score.

00:14:50.360 --> 00:14:55.280
For values of w that are very
far from my data,

00:14:55.280 --> 00:14:59.780
like w equals 100 in the
furthest right option here,

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:01.560
it should have a really bad
score.

00:15:01.560 --> 00:15:04.960
that indicates this is not a
good W.

00:15:04.960 --> 00:15:08.840
And for values of W that are
closer to most of the data,

00:15:08.840 --> 00:15:12.590
I should have good scores.

00:15:14.300 --> 00:15:18.920
What's one way I could try and
come up with such a scoring

00:15:18.920 --> 00:15:19.920
system?

00:15:21.780 --> 00:15:23.280
And to illustrate,

00:15:23.280 --> 00:15:27.640
let me actually simplify a
little and think of a smaller

00:15:27.640 --> 00:15:29.140
data set.

00:15:35.680 --> 00:15:38.780
of not the hundreds of commute
times in the scatter plot we

00:15:38.780 --> 00:15:40.120
were just looking at,

00:15:40.120 --> 00:15:43.870
but these five commute times.

00:15:44.060 --> 00:15:49.310
And I should say y1 equals 72.

00:16:03.480 --> 00:16:06.620
85 and y5 equals 92.

00:16:06.620 --> 00:16:08.420
By the way, if you see me
looking over at my computer,

00:16:08.420 --> 00:16:10.480
it's because I want to make sure
the numbers I write here are the

00:16:10.480 --> 00:16:13.140
same as the ones that are in the
notes so that the examples all

00:16:13.140 --> 00:16:15.940
are the same when you go back to
review.

00:16:15.940 --> 00:16:18.160
OK, let's pretend we have this
example data set of just five

00:16:18.160 --> 00:16:19.660
commute times.

00:16:20.840 --> 00:16:26.090
Why did I just write y1 through
y5? Where are the x's here?

00:16:31.880 --> 00:16:33.960
Where are the x's?

00:16:33.960 --> 00:16:34.400
Paul.

00:16:34.400 --> 00:16:37.860
It doesn't matter what they are
because it's not going to factor

00:16:37.860 --> 00:16:41.160
into our thinking of what the
best constant to predict is

00:16:41.160 --> 00:16:45.300
because the constant model
doesn't use x.

00:16:46.560 --> 00:16:49.400
Okay and so goal

00:16:49.400 --> 00:16:54.380
find the best

00:16:54.380 --> 00:17:00.380
w in h of x i equals w.

00:17:03.500 --> 00:17:04.500
Okay,

00:17:05.200 --> 00:17:08.180
given this small example,

00:17:08.180 --> 00:17:08.660
what is,

00:17:08.660 --> 00:17:13.910
pretend I pick a value of w.
Let's say I pick w equals 80.

00:17:15.340 --> 00:17:19.960
What's one way I could try and
measure how good or bad 80 is as

00:17:19.960 --> 00:17:21.560
a value of W.

00:17:21.560 --> 00:17:22.640
Yes, and remind me of your name?

00:17:22.640 --> 00:17:23.640
Sam.

00:17:24.000 --> 00:17:27.980
You could subtract each number
from 80.

00:17:28.240 --> 00:17:29.240
Exactly.

00:17:29.320 --> 00:17:32.880
The sort of initial thing we
might think of is just figure

00:17:32.880 --> 00:17:38.130
out how far each of the data
points is from our prediction.

00:17:38.340 --> 00:17:39.340
OK?

00:17:39.980 --> 00:17:41.480
And indeed,

00:17:42.980 --> 00:17:46.730
that's what we'll start with.

00:18:02.540 --> 00:18:04.180
So let's pick

00:18:04.180 --> 00:18:10.060
h of

00:18:10.060 --> 00:18:12.310
xi equals 80.

00:18:12.960 --> 00:18:15.680
Actually, before we get into the
specific example, So,

00:18:15.680 --> 00:18:20.180
let me define what error would
be.

00:18:34.220 --> 00:18:37.080
I should use orange for all of
my h of x i's because that's

00:18:37.080 --> 00:18:41.580
what I use in the notes.

00:18:50.360 --> 00:18:51.360
OK,

00:18:52.140 --> 00:18:55.160
so this is the definition of
error,

00:18:55.160 --> 00:18:58.640
yi minus h of xi.

00:18:58.640 --> 00:19:04.060
All this is is just the fancy
way of saying actual minus

00:19:04.060 --> 00:19:05.520
predicted.

00:19:07.460 --> 00:19:11.740
The yi's are just these five
things for each value of i from

00:19:11.740 --> 00:19:13.360
1 to 5 here.
I can compute this.

00:19:13.360 --> 00:19:16.440
And the h of xi is what I'm
predicting.

00:19:16.440 --> 00:19:19.480
And here, h of xi is just going
to be the same constant for all

00:19:19.480 --> 00:19:22.380
values in my data set.

00:19:22.380 --> 00:19:23.380
OK?

00:19:25.160 --> 00:19:28.160
Let's test this out.

00:19:56.680 --> 00:20:00.400
So then if I use h of xi equals
80,

00:20:00.400 --> 00:20:02.940
what is the error for the first
data point?

00:20:02.940 --> 00:20:03.940
y1.

00:20:04.140 --> 00:20:05.160
Yeah, remind me of your name.

00:20:05.160 --> 00:20:06.160
Ammar.

00:20:06.800 --> 00:20:07.800
Good.

00:20:08.080 --> 00:20:09.580
Minus a.

00:20:16.020 --> 00:20:20.520
What about for the second data
point?

00:20:22.320 --> 00:20:22.760
There,

00:20:22.760 --> 00:20:27.880
I'd have y2 minus h of x2 equals
what,

00:20:27.880 --> 00:20:28.880
Mar?

00:20:28.920 --> 00:20:31.920
I'd have positive 10.

00:20:35.300 --> 00:20:39.050
Let me do some highlighting
here.

00:20:39.600 --> 00:20:43.000
But it seems like there's a
problem here.

00:20:43.000 --> 00:20:45.250
What's the problem?

00:20:48.480 --> 00:20:54.100
Remember, my ultimate goal here
is to think of a way to measure

00:20:54.100 --> 00:20:59.780
the quality of a prediction in
which certain scores tell me a

00:20:59.780 --> 00:21:02.540
prediction was bad and other
scores tell me a prediction was

00:21:02.540 --> 00:21:03.480
good.

00:21:03.480 --> 00:21:05.440
But what's kind of going on
here?

00:21:05.440 --> 00:21:06.440
Sam?

00:21:06.480 --> 00:21:07.840
It's going to be 2,

00:21:07.840 --> 00:21:10.820
but it's really 18.
So it should be like,

00:21:10.820 --> 00:21:14.600
you don't want it to be
negative.

00:21:14.600 --> 00:21:17.620
Yeah, what we're getting at is
that it

00:21:17.620 --> 00:21:21.680
doesn't seem like these scores
should be able to be negative,

00:21:22.320 --> 00:21:23.120
OK?

00:21:23.120 --> 00:21:26.420
What would the best possible
prediction be for, let's say,

00:21:26.420 --> 00:21:27.300
y1?

00:21:27.300 --> 00:21:31.140
If I wanted my prediction to be
as good as possible for y1,

00:21:31.140 --> 00:21:33.660
what would the corresponding h
of x1 be?

00:21:33.660 --> 00:21:34.060
Bella?

00:21:34.060 --> 00:21:35.060
0.

00:21:35.460 --> 00:21:40.960
The error would be 0, exactly.
And so h of x1 would be

00:21:40.960 --> 00:21:43.900
72 for y1, because that's how we
would get the prediction

00:21:43.900 --> 00:21:44.900
perfect.

00:21:45.200 --> 00:21:47.400
And that would mean we would
have an error of 0.

00:21:47.400 --> 00:21:49.140
That's as good as we could do.

00:21:49.140 --> 00:21:53.340
What that tells us is values
closer to 0 should be better, or

00:21:53.340 --> 00:21:56.140
smaller values should be better.

00:21:56.140 --> 00:21:59.890
But negative 8 is technically
less than 10.

00:22:02.280 --> 00:22:03.280
Yeah.

00:22:07.020 --> 00:22:08.020
Actually,

00:22:08.400 --> 00:22:09.740
that example didn't work the way
I wanted to.

00:22:09.740 --> 00:22:11.140
But I think you see the point,

00:22:11.140 --> 00:22:15.640
which is that these scores
shouldn't be negative.

00:22:16.440 --> 00:22:19.140
Because if they could be
negative, and eventually what we

00:22:19.140 --> 00:22:22.460
are going to try and do is come
up with one aggregated or total

00:22:22.460 --> 00:22:24.460
score for the quality of the
prediction,

00:22:24.460 --> 00:22:26.800
the positive scores and the
negative scores could cancel

00:22:26.800 --> 00:22:29.400
out.
And what if I pick a prediction

00:22:29.400 --> 00:22:31.620
that's really terrible,

00:22:31.620 --> 00:22:35.200
like 61, for example?

00:22:35.400 --> 00:22:37.760
What's the error for 61?

00:22:37.760 --> 00:22:40.010
It's negative 19,

00:22:40.380 --> 00:22:42.980
which is really bad, but it's
the smallest number that would

00:22:42.980 --> 00:22:45.420
be on the screen, because
negative 19 is smaller than

00:22:45.420 --> 00:22:48.020
negative 8 and positive 10.

00:22:48.020 --> 00:22:51.160
So do we see the issue with
negative errors?

00:22:51.160 --> 00:22:52.400
Good.
So what we want to do is come up

00:22:52.400 --> 00:22:55.740
with a scoring system where
small values are consistently

00:22:55.740 --> 00:22:58.120
good and big values are
consistently bad.

00:22:58.120 --> 00:23:00.720
So we want to get rid of the
fact that some of these errors

00:23:00.720 --> 00:23:03.580
are positive and some are
negative.

00:23:03.580 --> 00:23:08.080
We want them to all be
non-negative.

00:23:14.280 --> 00:23:18.780
So what are some remedies to
that situation?

00:23:21.100 --> 00:23:22.100
Yeah,

00:23:22.620 --> 00:23:24.700
the most natural thing to do
would be to just take the

00:23:24.700 --> 00:23:26.700
absolute values of the errors.

00:23:26.700 --> 00:23:29.360
So they just can't be negative.

00:23:29.360 --> 00:23:31.420
The closer the actual was to
predicted,

00:23:31.420 --> 00:23:33.760
the smaller the absolute error
would be.

00:23:33.760 --> 00:23:35.980
The further away it is, the
larger the absolute error would

00:23:35.980 --> 00:23:36.760
be.

00:23:36.760 --> 00:23:40.760
That's a natural idea, but it's
actually not the idea we are

00:23:40.760 --> 00:23:41.580
going to use,

00:23:41.580 --> 00:23:44.500
at least not today.
We'll talk about that more on

00:23:44.500 --> 00:23:45.280
Tuesday.
OK,

00:23:45.280 --> 00:23:49.780
what are some other things we
could do?

00:23:51.780 --> 00:23:53.000
Remind me of your name?

00:23:53.000 --> 00:23:53.660
Emma.

00:23:53.660 --> 00:23:56.660
You could square it.
You could square it.

00:23:56.940 --> 00:23:57.820
Good.

00:23:57.820 --> 00:23:59.220
There are plenty of other things
you could do too.

00:23:59.220 --> 00:24:01.160
You could raise them to the
power of 4.

00:24:01.160 --> 00:24:03.800
You could do e to the power of
these things, because e to the

00:24:03.800 --> 00:24:06.320
power of something can't return
a negative number.

00:24:06.320 --> 00:24:09.060
There's lots of possible things
you could do.

00:24:09.060 --> 00:24:12.640
But squaring it turns out to be
the most convenient

00:24:12.640 --> 00:24:13.640
mathematically.

00:24:14.400 --> 00:24:17.680
And now you can maybe start to
see why we planted very specific

00:24:17.680 --> 00:24:19.660
questions in yesterday's lab.

00:24:19.660 --> 00:24:22.960
Okay if you remember yesterday's
lab worksheet there were

00:24:22.960 --> 00:24:26.120
functions that involved
something minus something

00:24:26.120 --> 00:24:28.600
squared and you had to take a
derivative.

00:24:28.600 --> 00:24:30.220
Why is that?

00:24:30.220 --> 00:24:32.420
Like why did we put that there?
Well it's because I knew we were

00:24:32.420 --> 00:24:35.760
going to talk about squaring the
errors here and at some point

00:24:35.760 --> 00:24:37.360
we're gonna have to take a
derivative.

00:24:37.360 --> 00:24:40.040
And the kind of thing you should
be thinking about is, well,

00:24:40.040 --> 00:24:44.120
our overall goal here is to find
the best w.

00:24:44.120 --> 00:24:47.400
So somehow we are going to pose
this as a calculus question, and

00:24:47.400 --> 00:24:49.360
derivatives are going to enter
the picture.

00:24:49.360 --> 00:24:53.860
And what do you know about
absolute values?

00:24:56.600 --> 00:24:58.880
What do you know about absolute
values?

00:24:58.880 --> 00:24:59.420
Yes,

00:24:59.420 --> 00:25:00.080
Andrew?

00:25:00.080 --> 00:25:01.960
The derivatives are not
continuous.

00:25:01.960 --> 00:25:04.420
Yeah, or the derivative isn't
defined everywhere.

00:25:04.420 --> 00:25:06.980
Exactly, because the absolute
value curve looks like this.

00:25:06.980 --> 00:25:10.880
And then at the point where
actual equals predicted, or

00:25:10.880 --> 00:25:13.220
where the input to the absolute
value is zero, there's no

00:25:13.220 --> 00:25:16.200
clearly defined derivative, so
it's a little harder to try and

00:25:16.200 --> 00:25:18.620
use calculus to figure out the
best w there.

00:25:18.620 --> 00:25:23.580
Which is why we are going to
start with squaring.

00:25:23.580 --> 00:25:28.080
And when we square our error,

00:25:28.480 --> 00:25:30.020
or take the absolute value,

00:25:30.020 --> 00:25:32.870
what we've produced is called a
loss function.

00:25:32.870 --> 00:25:35.870
A loss function measures

00:25:38.700 --> 00:25:42.870
how wrong or how good or bad

00:25:42.870 --> 00:25:45.120
a prediction is

00:25:47.040 --> 00:25:50.790
for a single data point.

00:25:55.190 --> 00:25:58.800
So the first loss function we
are going to see is called

00:25:58.800 --> 00:26:00.300
squared loss.

00:26:02.280 --> 00:26:05.020
Here's the formula for squared
loss.

00:26:05.020 --> 00:26:09.140
The letter I will use to refer
to a loss function

00:26:09.140 --> 00:26:13.640
is L with a subscript SQ,
standing for squared.

00:26:13.820 --> 00:26:16.500
And it's going to take in two
inputs,

00:26:16.500 --> 00:26:19.500
an actual y value,

00:26:19.800 --> 00:26:20.800
yi,

00:26:20.900 --> 00:26:22.660
and a predicted y value,

00:26:22.660 --> 00:26:24.300
h of xi.

00:26:24.300 --> 00:26:27.940
What's it going to return?

00:26:27.940 --> 00:26:32.440
What do I write on the
right-hand side here?

00:26:33.700 --> 00:26:36.940
Got it.
Would it be like yi minus h of x

00:26:36.940 --> 00:26:38.040
of i squared?

00:26:38.040 --> 00:26:38.460
Good.

00:26:38.460 --> 00:26:44.040
It's just actual y value minus
predicted y value

00:26:45.360 --> 00:26:46.360
squared.

00:26:47.000 --> 00:26:50.340
So when you see this, all you
should think is actual minus

00:26:50.340 --> 00:26:53.960
predicted squared.

00:26:59.340 --> 00:27:03.180
What do you think the other or
next loss function we will learn

00:27:03.180 --> 00:27:05.430
about is called?

00:27:08.760 --> 00:27:09.760
Yeah.

00:27:10.180 --> 00:27:13.930
Yeah, there's an absolute loss
as well.

00:27:16.210 --> 00:27:19.040
Its formula, as you might
predict,

00:27:19.040 --> 00:27:24.940
is still called L something, but
L abs takes in an actual y,

00:27:25.660 --> 00:27:28.680
predicted y,

00:27:29.200 --> 00:27:35.020
and returns the absolute value
of

00:27:35.020 --> 00:27:37.200
the difference.

00:27:43.780 --> 00:27:44.400
OK?

00:27:44.400 --> 00:27:46.480
Like I said, we're going to
start with squared loss because

00:27:46.480 --> 00:27:50.880
the squaring operation plays
nicely with calculus.

00:27:51.080 --> 00:27:53.940
But we will come back to
absolute loss Maybe briefly at

00:27:53.940 --> 00:27:57.900
the end of today's lecture, but
mostly on Tuesday.

00:27:57.900 --> 00:27:58.540
Okay,

00:27:58.540 --> 00:28:01.360
so now we have our like scoring
technique.

00:28:01.360 --> 00:28:03.940
We know how we are going to
evaluate the quality of a

00:28:03.940 --> 00:28:04.940
prediction

00:28:06.260 --> 00:28:11.510
for all points in our data set.

00:28:12.000 --> 00:28:15.750
So let's use squared loss.

00:28:18.100 --> 00:28:23.350
There's a visual I want to bring
in here.

00:28:25.940 --> 00:28:31.720
There we go.
What

00:28:31.720 --> 00:28:33.220
I've done,

00:28:37.220 --> 00:28:41.720
let me copy this as well.

00:28:51.660 --> 00:28:53.160
This happens.

00:28:55.820 --> 00:28:56.820
Okay.

00:28:58.920 --> 00:29:00.420
There we go.

00:29:00.420 --> 00:29:04.920
What I've done is drawn two
scatter plots.

00:29:04.960 --> 00:29:08.940
The X positions of the five
points in each are irrelevant.

00:29:08.940 --> 00:29:14.190
The Y positions are these five Y
values here.

00:29:14.280 --> 00:29:16.580
Do we see that? Like this is at
72,

00:29:16.580 --> 00:29:18.180
this is at 90,

00:29:18.180 --> 00:29:20.120
this is at 61.

00:29:20.120 --> 00:29:23.600
So it's the same data set that's
being visualized here.

00:29:23.600 --> 00:29:28.120
And what I'm doing is I'm
visualizing

00:29:28.620 --> 00:29:33.870
the squared loss for all five of
my data points

00:29:34.640 --> 00:29:39.140
for two different choices of w.

