The new name for it is mean
squared error.

01:02:02.410 --> 01:02:05.030
And the goal is to find the w
that makes it output the

01:02:05.030 --> 01:02:08.270
smallest possible number.
And that w will be the magical w

01:02:08.270 --> 01:02:09.990
that makes the best predictions

01:02:09.990 --> 01:02:12.790
What we said we would do is take
the derivative of it with

01:02:12.790 --> 01:02:15.040
respect to w

01:02:19.370 --> 01:02:22.090
So let's try and do that

01:02:22.090 --> 01:02:27.910
now as an aside as a side quest
what I want to do is maybe go to

01:02:27.910 --> 01:02:29.490
a different slide and

01:02:29.490 --> 01:02:33.240
Take the derivative of this
thing with respect to w

01:02:45.850 --> 01:02:48.770
The reason I want to do this
individual derivative separately

01:02:48.770 --> 01:02:53.350
is that once we have this, we
can use it to more easily find

01:02:53.350 --> 01:02:55.600
the overall derivative.

01:02:56.250 --> 01:03:00.730
So what's the derivative of yi
minus w squared

01:03:00.730 --> 01:03:03.730
with respect to w?

01:03:03.750 --> 01:03:08.470
What is it? Alex.
It would be negative 2 yi minus

01:03:08.470 --> 01:03:09.470
1.
Good.

01:03:09.530 --> 01:03:10.670
And how did you get there?

01:03:10.670 --> 01:03:11.850
I'm going to use a chain rule.

01:03:11.850 --> 01:03:13.330
Good.
So remember, the chain rule, the

01:03:13.330 --> 01:03:14.990
way that I like thinking about
it,

01:03:14.990 --> 01:03:18.690
is that the derivative of some
complicated expression

01:03:18.690 --> 01:03:21.690
with respect to w,

01:03:23.130 --> 01:03:27.230
maybe we'll say the derivative
of box to the power of n

01:03:27.790 --> 01:03:33.190
with respect to w, is equal to n
times box to the n minus 1

01:03:33.190 --> 01:03:36.940
times the derivative of what's
in the box directly.

01:03:37.130 --> 01:03:39.690
So here, that's how you'd use
the chain rule along with the

01:03:39.690 --> 01:03:41.190
power rule.

01:03:41.470 --> 01:03:43.170
Do we agree?

01:03:43.170 --> 01:03:44.850
That applied here,

01:03:44.850 --> 01:03:46.350
the n,

01:03:47.530 --> 01:03:49.370
no, this is not n, the number of
data points.

01:03:49.370 --> 01:03:51.370
Maybe I should have used a
different variable, but this is

01:03:51.370 --> 01:03:54.930
the rule you would have seen and
calculus would have used then.

01:03:54.930 --> 01:04:00.070
That applied here gives us 2
times yi minus w to the power of

01:04:00.070 --> 01:04:05.170
1, times the derivative of just
yi minus w.

01:04:07.550 --> 01:04:13.550
So I have 2 times yi minus w,
and what is this piece?

01:04:15.570 --> 01:04:17.050
Just 1.

01:04:17.050 --> 01:04:18.550
Not 1.

01:04:19.030 --> 01:04:20.150
Negative 1.

01:04:20.150 --> 01:04:20.710
Good.

01:04:20.710 --> 01:04:23.730
Because the coefficient on w is
negative 1,

01:04:23.730 --> 01:04:24.990
and this is sort of the tricky
thing.

01:04:24.990 --> 01:04:28.590
The yi is a constant from the
perspective of w,

01:04:28.590 --> 01:04:32.170
right? changing w does not
change the yi.

01:04:32.170 --> 01:04:34.510
So we multiply that by negative
1.

01:04:34.510 --> 01:04:36.850
And so we can collect out the
constants up front.

01:04:36.850 --> 01:04:42.100
So we have negative 2yi minus w.

01:04:42.170 --> 01:04:45.190
Note that since we are squaring
our loss,

01:04:45.190 --> 01:04:47.230
I like doing actual minus
predicted.

01:04:47.230 --> 01:04:49.910
But you could do predicted minus
actual squared.

01:04:49.910 --> 01:04:51.330
And you'd have the same thing.

01:04:51.330 --> 01:04:54.070
And this would be w minus yi
squared.

01:04:54.070 --> 01:04:58.570
And the derivative would be 2
times w minus yi.

01:04:59.230 --> 01:05:01.650
There wouldn't be the negative
in front, but I just like actual

01:05:01.650 --> 01:05:02.490
minus predicted,

01:05:02.490 --> 01:05:05.230
so that's what we'll use.

01:05:05.230 --> 01:05:06.230
OK.

01:05:06.830 --> 01:05:10.070
Why did I find this derivative
separately?

01:05:10.070 --> 01:05:15.320
How is that going to help me
here?

01:05:17.670 --> 01:05:18.950
What does that help with?

01:05:18.950 --> 01:05:21.070
Yeah, Kevin? Because when you're
doing a summation,

01:05:21.070 --> 01:05:24.390
you can treat the derivative to
the inside and it's the whole

01:05:24.390 --> 01:05:25.270
thing.
Exactly.

01:05:25.270 --> 01:05:28.690
Remember, one of the key rules
you learned in calc 1 is that if

01:05:28.690 --> 01:05:31.750
you have two functions being
added together,

01:05:31.750 --> 01:05:35.230
the derivative of the overall
sum is equal to the sum of their

01:05:35.230 --> 01:05:36.790
derivatives added up.

01:05:36.790 --> 01:05:39.730
So if you have f of x plus g of
x,

01:05:39.730 --> 01:05:43.810
that entire thing's derivative
is the derivative of f of x plus

01:05:43.810 --> 01:05:47.370
the derivative of g of x, which
is why all I really need to do

01:05:47.370 --> 01:05:48.350
here

01:05:48.350 --> 01:05:52.010
to apply the derivative
operation to the sum is just

01:05:52.010 --> 01:05:56.570
have a new sum made up of the
sum of the derivatives.

01:05:56.570 --> 01:05:57.130
OK.

01:05:57.130 --> 01:06:03.130
And then what do I do about our
1 over n friend up here?

01:06:06.450 --> 01:06:07.470
What do we do with that?

01:06:07.470 --> 01:06:08.990
It just stays there.
It stays there.

01:06:08.990 --> 01:06:10.950
It's a constant with respect to
w.

01:06:10.950 --> 01:06:12.690
So they can stay there.

01:06:12.690 --> 01:06:13.850
We're going to move into the
sum.

01:06:13.850 --> 01:06:17.230
and now what I'm going to
replace

01:06:17.230 --> 01:06:22.650
yi minus w with

01:06:22.650 --> 01:06:27.150
is the derivative with respect
to w of yi minus w squared

01:06:27.150 --> 01:06:32.810
that is the thing we found on
the previous page it's negative

01:06:32.810 --> 01:06:38.670
2 yi minus w and collecting

01:06:38.670 --> 01:06:41.510
like how can I Let's try this
further, actually.

01:06:41.510 --> 01:06:43.760
Let's ask that.

01:06:46.470 --> 01:06:49.470
You can pull the negative 2 out
outside of the summation.

01:06:49.470 --> 01:06:54.510
Good, the negative 2 is just a
constant being multiplied by

01:06:54.510 --> 01:06:57.770
every term in the sum.

01:06:57.770 --> 01:07:01.090
That's the same as just doing
the sum without the negative 2

01:07:01.090 --> 01:07:05.050
and multiplying the result
afterwards by negative 2, which

01:07:05.050 --> 01:07:07.650
is why I can pull the constant
up front.

01:07:07.650 --> 01:07:12.290
And that gives me negative 2
over n times the sum

01:07:12.290 --> 01:07:15.630
of yi minus w.

01:07:16.010 --> 01:07:19.590
So this is the derivative,

01:07:19.590 --> 01:07:20.550
OK?

01:07:20.550 --> 01:07:22.710
Perhaps looks different than the
derivatives you're used to

01:07:22.710 --> 01:07:25.650
calculating in calc 1, because
those probably didn't have

01:07:25.650 --> 01:07:27.090
summations in them.

01:07:27.090 --> 01:07:29.670
But this is the type of
derivative we will see a lot in

01:07:29.670 --> 01:07:30.930
this class,

01:07:30.930 --> 01:07:31.930
OK?

01:07:32.190 --> 01:07:33.450
OK, we got that.

01:07:33.450 --> 01:07:34.950
What's next?

01:07:35.290 --> 01:07:35.650
Yeah?

01:07:35.650 --> 01:07:38.390
Is that a 0? Yeah, all we need
to do now is set this derivative

01:07:38.390 --> 01:07:40.640
equal to zero.

01:07:42.070 --> 01:07:46.570
So let's write that one more
time.

01:07:59.410 --> 01:08:03.290
And now we're just going to set
this equal to zero and solve.

01:08:03.290 --> 01:08:06.290
So, let's copy this.

01:08:08.690 --> 01:08:13.190
What's the first thing we can
do?

01:08:15.650 --> 01:08:17.490
It's all for W, to be clear.

01:08:17.490 --> 01:08:19.430
What's the first thing we can do
to make that process a little

01:08:19.430 --> 01:08:20.430
simpler?

01:08:20.430 --> 01:08:20.730
Yeah.

01:08:20.730 --> 01:08:21.710
And remind us of your name?

01:08:21.710 --> 01:08:22.710
Chinmay.
Chinmay, right.

01:08:24.810 --> 01:08:27.270
Yeah, the negative 2 over N, we
can get rid of it.

01:08:27.270 --> 01:08:28.270
Why?

01:08:30.150 --> 01:08:31.150
Why?

01:08:32.770 --> 01:08:33.410
Awesome.

01:08:33.410 --> 01:08:35.970
Because if you multiply n by 0,
that's 0.

01:08:35.970 --> 01:08:37.390
And then divide by 2,

01:08:37.390 --> 01:08:38.390
that's 0.
Exactly.

01:08:38.390 --> 01:08:42.550
We could multiply by negative n
over 2,

01:08:42.550 --> 01:08:45.410
which is sort of what you'd have
to multiply negative 2 over n by

01:08:45.410 --> 01:08:47.830
to get 1 to get rid of the
constant.

01:08:47.830 --> 01:08:51.110
And on the right side, 0 times
negative n over 2 would still be

01:08:51.110 --> 01:08:53.410
0.
So it's just a way to free up

01:08:53.410 --> 01:08:55.650
some space and make the
expression a little clearer on

01:08:55.650 --> 01:08:57.900
the left-hand side.

01:08:59.010 --> 01:09:03.650
So I'll say multiply both sides

01:09:03.650 --> 01:09:07.470
by negative n over 2.

01:09:08.690 --> 01:09:11.770
Now, where did this over n come
from in the first place?

01:09:11.770 --> 01:09:14.990
Like, why is it there?

01:09:14.990 --> 01:09:15.410
Alex?

01:09:15.410 --> 01:09:16.970
Because we're taking the
average.

01:09:16.970 --> 01:09:18.630
We divide it all by the number
of days.

01:09:18.630 --> 01:09:21.490
Yeah, it's because we are
computing average squared loss

01:09:21.490 --> 01:09:25.370
or mean squared error.
If we instead did the sum of

01:09:25.370 --> 01:09:26.730
squared losses,

01:09:26.730 --> 01:09:29.190
we just wouldn't have the over
n.

01:09:29.190 --> 01:09:31.970
But this calculation for finding
the best w would be the same,

01:09:31.970 --> 01:09:37.650
because the over n dropped out
of our

01:09:37.650 --> 01:09:39.130
formula either way.

01:09:39.130 --> 01:09:42.630
So average squared loss or total
squared loss,

01:09:42.630 --> 01:09:46.130
the math ends up being the same
at this stage.

01:09:46.130 --> 01:09:48.250
By the way, how many people
here's name is Alex? I think

01:09:48.250 --> 01:09:50.090
there are at least three people
here.

01:09:50.090 --> 01:09:51.370
Yeah, OK, there are three
people.

01:09:51.370 --> 01:09:51.770
OK,

01:09:51.770 --> 01:09:56.210
that was confusing me a little,
But OK.

01:09:56.210 --> 01:09:59.210
Anyways, back to this.

01:10:02.090 --> 01:10:04.590
What we're trying to do now is
isolate for w.

01:10:04.590 --> 01:10:08.340
How can we do that?

01:10:09.590 --> 01:10:11.830
How can we isolate for w?

01:10:11.830 --> 01:10:12.490
Ammar?

01:10:12.490 --> 01:10:14.330
Just distribute the summation.

01:10:14.330 --> 01:10:15.330
Awesome.
I'll get to you next.

01:10:15.390 --> 01:10:18.070
I keep looking this way when I'm
asking for questions.

01:10:18.070 --> 01:10:18.390
Yeah.

01:10:18.390 --> 01:10:20.750
So how do I distribute the
summation? You can just split

01:10:20.750 --> 01:10:21.330
up,

01:10:21.330 --> 01:10:24.490
so the sum of y1 minus the sum
of w.

01:10:24.490 --> 01:10:25.250
Good.

01:10:25.250 --> 01:10:29.000
Whenever you have a summation,
and the thing being summed

01:10:29.150 --> 01:10:32.070
itself has an addition or
subtraction, you can distribute

01:10:32.070 --> 01:10:35.790
the sum operator across that.

01:10:35.790 --> 01:10:40.290
What that looks like here is
something like,

01:10:41.270 --> 01:10:46.520
let me free up some space here.

01:10:48.030 --> 01:10:52.530
Maybe I think this part was
clear.

01:10:55.490 --> 01:11:01.450
This is the sum of yi's minus
the

01:11:01.450 --> 01:11:04.610
sum of w's.

01:11:05.290 --> 01:11:09.190
How do I make this simpler?

01:11:09.190 --> 01:11:10.750
How do I make this simpler? Yes,
it reminds me of your name

01:11:10.750 --> 01:11:11.330
again?

01:11:11.330 --> 01:11:12.050
Gustavo.

01:11:12.050 --> 01:11:15.070
Gustavo.
So the w sum term, wouldn't it

01:11:15.070 --> 01:11:17.310
just be n times w? Good, why?

01:11:17.310 --> 01:11:19.290
Because you're just summing w n
times.

01:11:19.290 --> 01:11:21.510
That's just like w plus w plus w
would be just n times w.

01:11:21.510 --> 01:11:23.650
Exactly.
All this is doing is n times,

01:11:23.810 --> 01:11:25.810
it's adding the value of w.

01:11:25.810 --> 01:11:30.150
There's no subscript i here, so
the sum isn't any more

01:11:30.150 --> 01:11:32.110
complicated than that.
It's the sum of a constant n

01:11:32.110 --> 01:11:35.790
times, so this is just n times
w.

01:11:35.790 --> 01:11:41.710
And so simplifying that gives us

01:11:41.710 --> 01:11:46.960
the sum of yi's minus nw equals
zero.

01:11:47.050 --> 01:11:50.490
And now we're more or less done
because our goal was to isolate

01:11:50.490 --> 01:11:51.990
for w.

01:11:52.130 --> 01:11:55.530
On the left we have the sum of
yi's and on the right we have n

01:11:55.530 --> 01:11:57.030
times w.

01:11:57.850 --> 01:12:03.510
Now, finally, what do I need to
do to isolate for w?

01:12:06.070 --> 01:12:07.230
Divide by n.

01:12:07.230 --> 01:12:10.170
Yeah, I divide both sides by n,
and I'll reverse the order here

01:12:10.170 --> 01:12:13.130
just so it's a little more
natural.

01:12:13.130 --> 01:12:18.380
What this gives me is a formula
for what?

01:12:19.250 --> 01:12:20.490
It's not the mean.

01:12:20.490 --> 01:12:23.050
Yeah, it's the mean, or the
average, of the y's.

01:12:23.050 --> 01:12:27.970
And now I'm going to put a star
on this w because we've found

01:12:27.970 --> 01:12:29.850
that it is the best one,

01:12:29.850 --> 01:12:32.710
or it is the optimal w.

01:12:32.710 --> 01:12:34.250
And this is just,

01:12:34.250 --> 01:12:35.250
indeed,

01:12:39.050 --> 01:12:44.030
the average slash mean of

01:12:44.030 --> 01:12:46.370
the y's.

01:12:47.090 --> 01:12:49.270
And I think that's a pretty cool
result, because you already knew

01:12:49.270 --> 01:12:53.770
about the concept of an average
and a mean before this class.

01:12:54.210 --> 01:12:57.810
But now you know a little more
about it, which is that if your

01:12:57.810 --> 01:13:00.810
goal is to make a constant
prediction for all future data

01:13:00.810 --> 01:13:02.150
points based

01:13:02.150 --> 01:13:04.670
on some collection of numbers,

01:13:04.670 --> 01:13:08.330
the mean of the data is actually
the best constant prediction you

01:13:08.330 --> 01:13:09.630
can make.

01:13:09.630 --> 01:13:11.930
It's not just convenient, but
it's also the best.

01:13:11.930 --> 01:13:17.530
But it's the best under a very
specific lens, which is the lens

01:13:17.530 --> 01:13:22.130
in which you measure the quality
of predictions using squared

01:13:22.130 --> 01:13:23.130
loss.

01:13:24.230 --> 01:13:27.710
If we instead picked another way
to measure how good or bad a

01:13:27.710 --> 01:13:29.210
prediction is,

01:13:29.810 --> 01:13:32.810
the mean doesn't necessarily end
up still being the best.

01:13:32.810 --> 01:13:35.190
If we think of a different loss
function and a different way of

01:13:35.190 --> 01:13:38.230
quantifying how good or bad
predictions are,

01:13:38.230 --> 01:13:43.370
we may end up with a different
best W.

01:13:43.370 --> 01:13:46.370
Okay, so to recap,

01:13:48.890 --> 01:13:52.650
this function,

01:13:53.630 --> 01:13:59.630
1 over n times the sum of

01:13:59.630 --> 01:14:01.970
yi minus w squared,

01:14:01.970 --> 01:14:04.670
which went by many names, mean
squared error, average squared

01:14:04.670 --> 01:14:09.170
loss, both of those are also
called empirical risk,

01:14:09.690 --> 01:14:15.170
that function is minimized at

01:14:15.170 --> 01:14:18.030
w star equals

01:14:18.030 --> 01:14:23.420
the mean of of the

01:14:23.420 --> 01:14:24.420
y's.

01:14:24.940 --> 01:14:30.680
The star means w star is an

01:14:30.680 --> 01:14:35.080
optimal parameter.

01:14:38.760 --> 01:14:41.800
It's the star because it's
special.

01:14:41.800 --> 01:14:45.800
And so all of this was set up

01:14:45.800 --> 01:14:51.660
really to be able to come back
to this plot and say,

01:14:51.820 --> 01:14:55.260
well, now I actually know what
the best W to choose is.

01:14:55.260 --> 01:14:57.460
I know what the best horizontal
position

01:14:57.460 --> 01:14:59.640
to use for my constant
predictions is.

01:14:59.640 --> 01:15:04.880
It's the average of all of the y
values in my data set.

01:15:04.880 --> 01:15:06.160
Do we agree with that?

01:15:06.160 --> 01:15:10.840
You can't do any better if your
goal

01:15:10.840 --> 01:15:13.960
or if your sort of measuring
stick for what makes a good

01:15:13.960 --> 01:15:17.600
prediction is one that has low
average squared loss.

01:15:17.600 --> 01:15:20.700
If you think of a different
scoring system, well then maybe

01:15:20.700 --> 01:15:23.800
a different constant prediction
would end up being the best.

01:15:24.780 --> 01:15:29.680
To give you a preview of what's
to come on Tuesday,

01:15:29.680 --> 01:15:32.000
let's see if you can predict,

01:15:32.000 --> 01:15:33.740
I guess no pun intended,

01:15:33.740 --> 01:15:35.900
what would happen if we used a
different loss function.

01:15:35.900 --> 01:15:39.340
If we used absolute loss
instead,

01:15:39.340 --> 01:15:41.740
the best prediction isn't the
mean.

01:15:41.740 --> 01:15:45.860
But what is it, if you have to
guess?

01:15:45.860 --> 01:15:48.860
It's not the mean.

01:15:49.180 --> 01:15:50.600
It's the median,

01:15:50.600 --> 01:15:51.600
yeah.

01:15:51.840 --> 01:15:55.000
We will work through why that's
the case on Tuesday and you

01:15:55.000 --> 01:15:56.900
should read a little bit of
chapter 1.3

01:15:56.900 --> 01:15:58.520
to start to see why that's the
case.

01:15:58.520 --> 01:16:01.060
But my point is that the mean is
the best constant prediction.

01:16:01.060 --> 01:16:04.460
That's a nice one-sentence
summary, but it's assuming we

01:16:04.460 --> 01:16:07.120
use squared loss as our loss
function.

01:16:07.120 --> 01:16:07.640
Okay,

01:16:07.640 --> 01:16:10.000
now to get us thinking about
what it would look like if we

01:16:10.000 --> 01:16:15.640
used other loss functions, But
what I want to write

01:16:15.640 --> 01:16:19.740
down, and this is what we will
start with on Tuesday,

01:16:19.740 --> 01:16:24.860
is what I call the three-step
modeling recipe,

01:16:25.280 --> 01:16:27.720
which summarizes everything
we've done

01:16:28.220 --> 01:16:31.560
and shows us how we could repeat
these steps,

01:16:31.560 --> 01:16:35.040
but with maybe different models
and different loss functions.

01:16:35.040 --> 01:16:39.560
The three steps we followed
today We're to choose a model,

01:16:43.040 --> 01:16:46.040
choose a loss function.

01:16:49.360 --> 01:16:53.140
Let me move this up a little.

01:16:53.140 --> 01:16:57.500
And the third thing we did was
we minimized

01:16:57.500 --> 01:17:00.420
average loss

01:17:00.420 --> 01:17:06.160
to find optimal

